{
	title: 'Startup budget: UI in 100 ms, typing in 200 ms',
	description: `\`./run\` in a terminal draws the UI (tab bar, prompt with the local draft, the focused tab's tail) within 100 ms of starting and accepts typed input within 200 ms, with 50 open tabs and sessions with long histories (at least one 20 MB), whether it becomes host or joins one. The first frame does not wait for anything it does not show: recovering unfinished turns, provider model lists, the web endpoint, credential refresh and background tabs run after it. Keys typed before the host answers go into the editor and are sent when connected (drafts already allow that). ./run passes its start time (HAL_STARTUP_TIMESTAMP, milliseconds since the epoch) so measurements include Bun's own start, and a small perf module keeps timestamped marks in memory, shown by /perf (artifact old-perf.ts).
A test enforces the budget: it builds a temp home with the tabs and histories above, starts ./run under a pseudo-terminal (Bun.Terminal), and fails if the first prompt frame arrives after 100 ms or a typed character is not echoed in the frame within 200 ms; it takes the median of several starts, both as host and joining a running host. The test states its machine assumption and is skipped, with a printed reason, where no PTY exists; it is never loosened to pass.`,
